# The test-count ratchet: the FLOOR for the two suites this repo owns.
#
# WHY A FLOOR AND NOT A DOCUMENT
# ------------------------------
# The gate ran the policy suite and the integration suite and reported the
# tool's exit code. Deleting a test case therefore left it GREEN — a smaller
# suite still passes. "159 tests should pass" written in a checklist is not a
# ratchet; nothing reads it. These numbers are read by `stage_ratchet` in
# harden/verify.sh, which RE-DERIVES both counts from the suites as they exist
# right now (two independent derivations each, which must agree) and fails when
# the live count is under the floor.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE THEM
# ------------------
# Raising a floor: add tests, run `bash harden/verify.sh` (the stage prints the
# new number), commit the higher value. That is the ratchet moving.
#
# Lowering a floor: the stage REFUSES it. It reads every value this file has
# ever held in git history and fails when the working value is below the
# highest one, because "the gate went red so I edited the number down" is
# exactly the failure this file exists to prevent. A genuinely smaller suite
# (a module extracted to its own repo, say) needs the removal reviewed on its
# own merits, so it must be recorded here as an explicit, greppable line:
#
#   allow_lower=<key>:<old>:<new>:<reason>
#
# which the stage honours ONCE for that exact old->new step and announces in
# capitals on every subsequent run. There is no quiet way down.
#
# policies    — cases executed by `cerbos compile policies/`, i.e. the
#               principals x resources x actions cross-product of every
#               `tests[].input` block under policies/*/*_test.yaml.
# integration — tests that actually PASS in `pytest integration/tests`.
#               Passing, not collected: a test turned into a skip stops
#               asserting, and this floor notices.
policies=191
integration=77
